Vulkan engine backend: batch the GPU work instead of waiting on every operation.

Every draw, upload, clear and target set-up was its own command buffer, submitted and waited on, and every draw its
own render pass. A fight in Soulcalibur II HD issues several hundred draws a frame, and on Windows the frame thread
spent its time in the driver's fence wait (three frames a second). Draws now record into one open command buffer,
consecutive draws into the same framebuffer share a render pass (a global memory barrier between passes), and
uploads, clears, target creation and depth snapshots are recorded into the same batch. Vertices, indices,
constants, staging and descriptor sets come from per-batch arenas; textures released while the batch may still use
them are destroyed after it. The batch is submitted and waited on only for a readback, a present, the engine's
submit_and_wait, or before a framebuffer, pipeline or render target goes away.

